---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import MappingDemo from '../components/MappingDemo.astro';
import ActionDemo from '../components/ActionDemo.astro';
import InterfaceMap from '../components/InterfaceMap.astro';
import '../styles/catalog.css';
---

<Base
  title="From email to service"
  theme="light"
  description="Explore the service interfaces around MAP, then follow a publication decision through worked HTTP and MCP exchanges."
>
  <main id="main" class="container example-page">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-line">The protocol at work</span>
      <h1>From email to service.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The email describes the decision. A connection you approved reads the current proposal from
        the service and submits your choice through the interface the service already provides.
      </p>
    </header>
    <InterfaceMap />
    <section class="example-notes">
      <div>
        <h2>Different paths. The same decision.</h2>
        <p>
          A service may expose an HTTP operation, a tool or another authenticated interface. In
          every case, it must know who is acting, which proposal they saw and whether they are still
          allowed to make that choice.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/interfaces">
          Read the full sourced assessment →
        </a>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Reuse what the service already has.</h2>
        <p>
          Keep the service's existing operations, authentication and result format. An installed
          binding identifies which operation matches the type. A description cannot make an unsafe
          operation safe: the service must check permission and the proposal version when it acts.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/specification/bindings">
          How to document a binding →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="worked-examples" id="worked-examples">
      <span class="eyebrow eyebrow-line">Worked exchanges</span>
      <h2>Inspect one publication decision.</h2>
      <p>
        These HTTP and MCP illustrations use the same proposal, service read and exact revision.
        They show the steps in code. The wider research catalogue records the requirements for other
        interfaces.
      </p>
      <MappingDemo />
    </section>
    <section class="example-notes">
      <div>
        <h2>About the artifacts</h2>
        <p>
          The description, content, native API example and MCP exchange are generated from one set
          of source artifacts. Changing the contract regenerates their identity and digest together.
          The OpenAPI document describes the illustrative service, with no custom MAP mapping
          language.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>What they establish</h2>
        <p>
          These exchanges explain the design; they make no live service calls or interoperability
          claim. The MIME download is unsigned. MCP initialization and account setup are omitted.
          Actual implementations need evidence for the service's authorization, version check and
          result interpretation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="example-guardrails">
      <h2>Try a decision after the terms change.</h2>
      <p>
        For a campaign approval, change the audience, revoke permission or expire the proposal
        between the service read and confirmation. This local model shows why the service must check
        again when it acts.
      </p>
      <ActionDemo />
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
<style>
  .worked-examples {
    padding: 25px 0 0;
    scroll-margin-top: 110px;
  }
  .worked-examples > h2 {
    font-size: clamp(26px, 3vw, 36px);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin: 18px 0;
  }
  .worked-examples > p {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: oklch(43% 0.014 275);
    margin-bottom: 30px;
  }
  .example-guardrails {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-light);
    padding: 60px 0 80px;
  }
  .example-guardrails > h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 36px);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }
  .example-guardrails > p {
    color: #746d7e;
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
  }
</style>
